Sliding Window Maximum (LC 239)

// You are given an integer array nums
// and an integer k.
// Return an array of the maximum value
// in each sliding window of size k.
// Formula:
// window = [i − k + 1 ... i]
// max = front of deque

// Rules:
// - Window size is fixed (k)
// - Need maximum for each window
// - Must be efficient (better than O(nk))
/ Pattern
// Monotonic Deque (Decreasing Order)

// When to Use (Trigger)
// - Sliding window + need max/min
// - Repeated window queries
// - Optimization over brute force

// ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Core Invariant
// ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Deque stores indices
// Values in deque are in decreasing order
// Front always holds index of current maximum

// Pointer Rules (Measurement Logic)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────
// For each index i:
// - Remove indices out of window (i - k)
// - Remove smaller elements from back
// - Add current index to deque
// - If window formed (i >= k - 1):
// result = nums[deque.peekFirst()]

// ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Time & Space
// ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Time: O(n) → each element added & removed once
// Space: O(k)
